#
# The build context of examples/deploy-agent/Dockerfile. BuildKit reads this
# file, named after the Dockerfile and beside it, in place of the root
# .dockerignore, so it travels with the Dockerfile when the two are copied into
# another project (audit A23: a copied Dockerfile lost the root file's rules).
# Paths are relative to the context, the directory `docker build` is given.

# Build output: the image compiles everything itself.
**/target

# Secrets and local configuration a developer keeps beside the source: none
# belongs in a build context, where every stage that copies the tree keeps it
# in a layer and in the build cache (audit A12).
**/.env
**/.env.*
**/*.pem
**/*.key
**/*.p12
**/*.pfx

# History and editor state.
.git
.github
**/.DS_Store
**/.idea
**/.vscode

# What no build of this image reads. Left in, an edit to any of it changed the
# `COPY . .` layer and rebuilt the workspace's own crates with link-time
# optimisation, minutes for a typo in the book (audit A19). Everything a
# workspace member's manifest or build script needs stays: crates/, examples/,
# benches/, book-tests/, tck/, tools/, the root Cargo.toml and Cargo.lock.
/*.md
/*.cff
acts
bindings
book
deploy
docs
fuzz
itk
packaging
proto
scripts
spec
verification
